ask(1926,'learned-risk','Failure Modes of Learned Models bullets','A robot trained in simulation encounters unfamiliar friction and chooses an unsuitable maneuver. Which two source risks are directly implicated?', [('A simulation-to-real dynamics mismatch and behavior outside the training distribution','Both concern a policy encountering conditions different from its training experience.'),('A low message compression ratio and a fixed maintenance schedule','These do not explain the stated learned dynamics mismatch.'),('A larger training reward and a successful nominal validation run','Neither observation rules out mismatch or out-of-distribution failure.')],kind='scenario')
ask(1926,'learned-risk','Confidently wrong perception bullet','An unfamiliar object is classified with high model confidence but an incorrect label. Which interpretation matches the source?', [('High confidence is not sufficient evidence of correctness on unfamiliar inputs','The slide explicitly warns that neural perception can be confidently wrong.'),('The confidence score proves the ground-truth label must be mistaken','Model confidence does not override independent ground truth.'),('Only low-confidence outputs can be out-of-distribution errors','The source directly contradicts that restriction.')])
ask(1926,'learned-risk','Deep RL interpretability comparison bullet','What is the stated reason to keep learned control behavior auditable through additional safety mechanisms?', [('Deep RL decisions can be harder to interpret than the structure of classical PID or LQR controllers','The source contrasts interpretability, without proving classical controllers cannot fail.'),('PID and LQR require no model assumptions, tuning or validation','The interpretability comparison does not remove classical controllers’ assumptions or validation needs.'),('A more complex learned policy is automatically easier to explain because it has more parameters','Parameter count alone does not provide interpretable decision structure.')])
RULE=fm('accept = (u ≤ u_max) ∧ (c ≥ c_min); otherwise use the supplied fallback',{'accept':'Boolean permission for the proposed action','u':'proposed action magnitude','u_max':'supplied maximum permitted magnitude','c':'supplied confidence score','c_min':'supplied minimum confidence score'},{'accept':'Boolean','u':'arbitrary control units','u_max':'arbitrary control units','c':'dimensionless','c_min':'dimensionless'},['This is a supplied educational decision rule, not a certified physical safety algorithm.','Action magnitude is nonnegative.','The inequalities include equality; both conditions must hold.','Confidence alone is not a guarantee of perception correctness.'])
ask(1927,'risk-mitigation','Safety envelopes and uncertainty-fallback bullets','Use this supplied toy rule: accept a proposed action only if its magnitude u ≤ 2 control units and confidence c ≥ 0.8; otherwise invoke a predefined fallback. Proposals are P=(2,0.8), Q=(2.1,0.99), R=(1,0.7). Which are accepted by this rule?', [('P only','P satisfies both inclusive bounds; Q violates the action limit and R violates the confidence condition.'),('P and Q','This lets high confidence override the independent action-magnitude limit.'),('None','This incorrectly treats the inclusive boundary values in P as failures.')],kind='algorithm_trace',ass=RULE['assumptions'],steps=['P:2≤2 and0.8≥0.8 are both true.','Q:2.1≤2 is false despite high confidence.','R:0.7≥0.8 is false despite the small action.','Accept only P; physical safety requires additional analysis outside this toy rule.'],formulas=[RULE],cites=[(1926,'Confidently wrong perception warning')],conflict=CONF)
ask(1927,'risk-mitigation','Domain randomization and human override bullets','Which plan best reflects the mitigation list while respecting its limits?', [('Vary simulation conditions during training, retain an overriding safety envelope and provide human intervention for high-consequence decisions','The listed methods reduce different risks and are complementary rather than a proof that all transfer failures vanish.'),('Use one exact simulator calibration and remove override after the nominal policy succeeds','A single calibration does not cover varied real conditions, and nominal success does not justify removing the listed safeguards.'),('Rely on high confidence alone, because randomized training eliminates simulation-to-real mismatch','Randomization can narrow the gap but does not guarantee its elimination or confidence correctness.')],kind='scenario',conflict=CONF)
ask(1928,'standards-risk','Established Standards three-bullet list','Which course-level standards mapping is appropriate, with scope qualified by the cited ISO catalogs?', [('ISO10218: industrial robots and integration; ISO/TS15066: collaborative industrial operation; ISO13482: personal care robots','This retains the course mapping while limiting the collaborative specification to its industrial scope.'),('ISO10218: personal care; ISO/TS15066: general medical diagnosis; ISO13482: industrial arm integration','These scopes are exchanged or introduced without support.'),('ISO10218: collaborative operation only; ISO/TS15066: all consumer privacy; ISO13482: industrial welding only','This substitutes narrower or unrelated domains for the stated scopes.')],kind='match',conflict=STD)
ask(1928,'standards-risk','Risk Assessment Process ordered bullets','Which order matches the lecture’s simplified risk-assessment process?', [('Identify full-envelope hazards → estimate severity/likelihood → select controls → test mitigations and revisit after change','Hazard identification and assessment inform control selection and validation.'),('Select a warning label → assume residual risk is low → identify hazards only after deployment','This reverses the stated assessment and validation sequence.'),('Test only the nominal task → freeze the assessment permanently → assess hazards after a redesign','The source requires the full envelope and revision after design changes.')],kind='sequence',ass=['This is the slide’s simplified process, not a complete compliance procedure.'])
ask(1928,'standards-risk','Hierarchy of controls bullet','If a hazard can feasibly be eliminated by redesign, which choice best follows the source’s hierarchy?', [('Consider eliminating the hazard before relying on guards, warnings or training','The source places elimination before the later control layers.'),('Prefer operator training first because it leaves the mechanical design unchanged','Convenience does not reverse the stated priority of control effectiveness.'),('Treat a warning label as equivalent to eliminating the hazardous mechanism','A warning communicates a hazard; elimination removes its source.')],kind='scenario')
ask(1928,'standards-risk','Validation and design-change bullet','An arm receives a new tool that changes its reach and contact behavior. What should happen to the prior risk assessment?', [('Revisit the changed hazards and validate the relevant mitigations again','The new task envelope and interaction behavior can invalidate earlier assumptions.'),('Retain the old assessment solely because the controller software version is unchanged','Mechanical changes can alter risk without a software change.'),('Update only the product brochure because the original robot passed testing','Past validation is bounded by its tested configuration, not every future tool.')],kind='scenario')
ask(1929,'safety-culture','Near-miss and boundary-testing bullets','A robot unexpectedly passes close to a person without causing harm. What response follows the lecture’s safety-culture guidance?', [('Log and investigate the near miss, including relevant boundary conditions','A no-harm event can reveal a failure path worth correcting before injury occurs.'),('Exclude it from review because only harmful events count as safety evidence','The source specifically includes near misses and unexpected behavior.'),('Improve nominal-case scores first and defer the unusual condition until after rollout','The source calls for explicit boundary testing, not only typical performance.')],kind='scenario')
ask(1929,'safety-culture','Staged deployment and independent layers bullets','Which rollout plan preserves both staged evidence and independent safety layers?', [('Simulation → constrained pilot → full rollout, with gates at each stage and mechanical, software and procedural measures','The source pairs staged validation with multiple kinds of safety layer.'),('Simulation success → immediate full rollout, followed by an optional pilot if complaints appear','This skips the constrained pilot and prospective gate.'),('A single learned controller replaces the mechanical and procedural layers once its test accuracy improves','The recommended layers are complementary, not all reducible to one learned component.')],kind='sequence')
ask(1930,'technical-frontiers','Foundation Models for Robotics bullet','What is the stated aim of vision-language-action foundation models?', [('Generalize robotic behavior across tasks using large pretrained multimodal models','The source describes an aim of cross-task generalization, not an already universal capability.'),('Predict language tokens only, without connecting perception to action','That omits the vision and action aspects of the named model family.'),('Store one fixed trajectory for each exact object with no cross-task transfer','That is task-specific replay rather than the stated generalization direction.')])
ask(1930,'technical-frontiers','Multi-Agent Coordination and Soft & Bio-Inspired Robotics bullets','Which pairing follows the two emerging directions?', [('Robot fleets: learned communication/shared policies; soft robots: compliant materials combined with learned control','The source links coordination to fleet interaction and physical adaptation to materials plus control.'),('Robot fleets: central control is the only possible coordination; soft robots: material choice replaces all control','Both impose exclusions the source does not make.'),('Robot fleets: each robot ignores other agents; soft robots: rigidity alone supplies the stated compliance','These remove the defining interaction and compliance characteristics.')],kind='match')
ask(1931,'transfer-active-learning','Active Perception bullet','A robot moves its camera to see behind an occluding object before choosing a grasp. Why is this active perception?', [('It chooses a sensing action to obtain more informative observations','The action changes what can be observed rather than only processing a fixed image.'),('It applies a larger classifier to the same unchanged view','That may improve processing, but does not describe the deliberate sensing action.'),('It executes the grasp first and labels the outcome after contact','That is not the described pre-action view selection for information.')],kind='scenario')
ask(1931,'transfer-active-learning','Sim-to-Real at Scale and Few-Shot Skill Learning bullets','Which distinction matches the two learning directions?', [('Sim-to-real methods address transfer across simulated/real conditions; few-shot methods aim to learn a skill from few demonstrations','They concern environmental transfer and demonstration efficiency respectively.'),('Few-shot learning means the controller executes only a few timesteps regardless of training data','Few-shot concerns learning examples, not a fixed execution horizon.'),('Differentiable simulation means simulated predictions cannot differ from real dynamics','Differentiability facilitates optimization; it does not prove perfect physical fidelity.')])
ask(1932,'future-trajectory','Opening qualifier and near/mid/longer-term bullets','How should the lecture’s near-, mid- and longer-term robotics trajectory be interpreted?', [('As conditional directions, with household humanoid adoption depending on cost, safety and dexterity thresholds','The opening explicitly says directional, not predictive, and the longer-term scenario is conditional.'),('As guaranteed product availability dates measured from the date a learner reads the slide','No such dated commitment is made.'),('As evidence that regulation should wait until all technical deployment is complete','The horizon statement says norms and regulation mature alongside the technology.')],conflict=FORE)
ask(1932,'future-trajectory','Near Term and Mid Term bullets','Which contrast is actually drawn between near- and mid-term directions?', [('Near term emphasizes broader industrial cobot/AMR use; mid term emphasizes more general manipulation with less task-specific programming','These are the two source-listed directional emphases.'),('Near term promises household humanoids everywhere; mid term removes the need for safety validation','Neither is the stated sequence or condition.'),('Near term is only regulation; mid term is only declining battery density','These are not the specified technology trajectories.')],ass=['The categories are illustrative directions, not forecasts or dates.'])
ask(1933,'extreme-future','Novel mechanisms and infrastructure monitoring bullets','Which mechanical/task pairing matches the frontier examples?', [('Cable-driven parallel mechanisms: large-workspace manipulation; wall-climbing robots: infrastructure inspection','These retain the specific mechanism and application associations.'),('Cable-driven mechanisms: speech transcription; wall-climbing robots: medical-image interpretation','These unrelated functions do not express the named mechanical advantages.'),('Cable-driven mechanisms: only fixed-point sensing; wall-climbing robots: only flat-floor warehouse transport','These unnecessarily remove the large-workspace and wall-access capabilities described.')],kind='match')
ask(1933,'extreme-future','Space and disaster-response bullets','Why are both local decision-making and robust perception important in the extreme-environment examples?', [('Delayed communication limits timely human response, while unpredictable scenes challenge observation quality','The source identifies distinct control-delay and perception challenges.'),('Long communication delay improves the accuracy of each camera measurement','Delay does not itself improve sensing.'),('Robust image classification by itself removes the need for locally executable control','Reliable observations still must lead to timely actions when remote response is unavailable.')],kind='scenario')
ask(1934,'deployment-opportunities','Transfer learning and open-source framework bullets','Which opportunity pairing follows the lecture?', [('Transfer learning can reduce adaptation data needs; open-source frameworks can accelerate integration and development','These address model adaptation and reusable software infrastructure, without guaranteeing zero work.'),('Transfer learning eliminates task-specific validation; open-source licensing guarantees deployment correctness','Neither benefit establishes an unconditional validity guarantee.'),('Transfer learning is only hardware reuse; ROS is only a collection of pretrained weights','This misidentifies learned-model transfer and robotics software infrastructure.')])
ask(1934,'deployment-opportunities','Falling costs and hybrid-compute bullets','A source reports falling compute costs, but a robot still has a strict response deadline. Which design conclusion is justified?', [('Consider affordability and model capability separately from measured end-to-end latency when choosing cloud versus edge placement','Lower cost or stronger remote computation does not by itself meet a timing requirement.'),('Choose cloud processing solely from its lower compute price, without assessing network delay','That ignores the latency side of the stated trade-off.'),('Assume edge processing is always slower because its model is smaller','Model size alone does not determine end-to-end timing, especially with communication.')],kind='scenario',ass=['The cost trend is attributed to the lecture, not a current price quote.'])
ENERGY=fm('E_use = (1-r)E_nom; P_total = P_drive + P_compute; t = E_use/P_total',{'E_use':'usable battery energy','r':'reserved fraction of nominal energy','E_nom':'nominal battery energy','P_total':'constant total load power','P_drive':'drive power','P_compute':'compute power','t':'ideal operating time'},{'E_use':'Wh','r':'dimensionless','E_nom':'Wh','P_total':'W','P_drive':'W','P_compute':'W','t':'h'},['Supplied nominal energy is225 Wh and reserve fraction0.20.','Drive and compute loads are constant60 W and30 W respectively.','Other loads, conversion loss, aging and power transients are excluded by the supplied toy model.'])
ask(1935,'deployment-challenges','Energy density and battery-life bullet','A supplied mobile-robot model has 225 Wh nominal energy, reserves 20%, and draws a constant 60 W for driving plus 30 W for computation. Does its ideal usable runtime cover a 2.2 h mission?', [('No: usable energy is 180 Wh and runtime is 2 h','With90 W total load,180/90=2 h, below the2.2 h requirement.'),('Yes: runtime is 2.5 h','225/90=2.5 h ignores the reserved20%.'),('Yes: runtime is 3 h','180/60=3 h ignores the30 W compute load.')],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',ass=ENERGY['assumptions'],steps=['Usable energy:(1−0.20)×225=180 Wh.','Total load:60+30=90 W.','Ideal usable runtime:180 Wh/90 W=2 h.','Compare2 h with2.2 h; the mission exceeds the toy energy budget.'],formulas=[ENERGY])
