ask(1935,'deployment-challenges','Long-horizon reasoning and planning bullet','In a supplied illustrative mission, all 10 steps must succeed and each independently succeeds with probability 0.98. What is the mission success probability?', [('About 81.71%','The required joint success probability is0.98^10≈0.81707, lower than an individual step’s probability.'),('98%','This substitutes a single-step success probability for the all-steps event.'),('80% exactly','Subtracting10×0.02 is a first-order failure approximation, not the exact independent-step probability.')],kind='numerical',ass=REL['assumptions'],steps=['Require the intersection of10 successful independent steps.','Multiply the10 equal probabilities:0.98^10≈0.8170728069.','Convert to percent:81.70728069%, or81.71%.'],formulas=[REL])
ask(1935,'deployment-challenges','Generalization and trust/public-acceptance bullets','A robot succeeds on its familiar test routes but fails on novel clutter, while users remain reluctant to share space with it. Which paired open challenges are illustrated?', [('Generalization beyond familiar environments and social trust/acceptance','The source treats technical robustness and acceptance as concurrent challenges.'),('Only battery capacity, because all autonomy failures are energy problems','The described perception/behavior and trust issues are not explained solely by energy.'),('Only publicity, because familiar-route success proves generalization','Success on familiar routes does not establish robustness to new scenes.')],kind='scenario')
