ask(1936,'course-review','Weeks1–2, Week3 and Week4 review bullets','A manipulator task supplies a desired tool pose, then requires a smooth motion and actuator effort estimates. Which allocation follows the course review?', [('Inverse kinematics selects compatible joint positions; trajectory generation specifies their time evolution; dynamics relates motion to forces/torques','The course separates geometry, time parameterization and dynamic effort.'),('Inverse kinematics alone determines the unique torque history without masses or timing','Pose compatibility does not supply inertial parameters or a time history.'),('A trajectory’s final pose alone determines every intermediate joint velocity and actuator torque','Boundary geometry does not fix an entire trajectory and its dynamics.')],kind='scenario',ass=['This is a supplied integration task, not a new numerical model.'],cites=[(1937,'Week5 control and Week6 learning review bullets')])
ask(1937,'course-review','Control, machine-learning and milestone bullets','A planned arm motion is disturbed during execution, while a separate model classifies grasp outcomes. Which separation is appropriate?', [('Feedback control regulates the disturbed motion; the learning model can inform subsequent decisions','The review connects modeling, control and learning without making them interchangeable.'),('The grasp classifier’s label automatically acts as the required continuous feedback law','Outcome classification does not by itself define motion regulation.'),('A previously generated trajectory eliminates the need to respond to disturbances','An open planned motion does not inherently correct deviations.')],kind='scenario',cites=[(1936,'Dynamics and trajectory-generation review bullets')])
ask(1938,'course-review','Weeks7–9 perception, navigation and localization review bullets','In a navigating service robot, which chain keeps the reviewed perception and navigation responsibilities distinct?', [('Use observations to estimate the scene/pose, select a route, and avoid obstacles while executing it','The review combines vision, localization/SLAM and planning/avoidance as complementary functions.'),('Treat every detected object label as a complete globally localized collision-free route','Detection alone is not localization plus route planning.'),('Treat a planned geometric route as a new measurement of the robot’s current pose','A plan is not an independent localization observation.')],kind='scenario',cites=[(1939,'Week10 interaction and Week11 sensing/integration bullets')])
ask(1939,'course-review','Week10 and Week11 review bullets','A robot understands a spoken destination but its wheel actuators do not follow the motion command. Which distinction matters?', [('Language understanding specifies intent; sensing, actuation and control still realize the physical task','The review’s HRI and hardware modules address different parts of a complete robot.'),('A correctly transcribed destination proves actuator execution is correct','Intent recognition is not evidence of actual motion.'),('Replacing the transformer necessarily repairs an actuator interface fault','The described failure is downstream of the correctly understood request.')],kind='scenario')
ask(1940,'core-foundations','State-space and linear-algebra/probability bullets','Which statement captures the mathematical connections emphasized in the course review?', [('State descriptions connect classical control and RL; linear algebra and probability support learning and SLAM','These are recurring mathematical structures rather than isolated course topics.'),('State-space models are restricted to classical control and cannot describe an RL agent’s environment','The source explicitly identifies a connection to RL framing.'),('Probability becomes irrelevant once a geometric kinematic model is available','A geometric model does not eliminate uncertain sensing or probabilistic learning/localization.')])
ask(1940,'core-foundations','Spatial representation and recurring dynamics bullets','A team changes from an arm to a mobile robot. Which foundations can still be relevant even though the mechanism differs?', [('Spatial representations and motion models, with dynamics adapted to the new mechanism','The source emphasizes recurring mathematical foundations, not identical parameters or equations for every robot.'),('The old arm’s exact joint-coordinate list and inertia matrix can be reused unchanged merely because both are robots','Shared principles do not imply identical mechanism-specific models.'),('All spatial-frame conventions should be discarded because only arms use coordinate transforms','Mobile robots also require consistent spatial representation.')],kind='scenario')
ask(1941,'core-learning','Reinforcement-learning contexts and computer-vision applications bullets','What is the appropriate inference from RL appearing in grasping, path planning and navigation, and vision appearing in detection and SLAM?', [('The learning/perception frameworks recur across tasks, but their states, actions, observations and objectives may need task-specific definitions','A shared methodological theme is not proof one unchanged policy or representation fits every application.'),('A trained grasping policy can be deployed unchanged for navigation because both are labeled RL','The state/action and task semantics can differ.'),('An object detector alone provides every map, pose estimate and navigation decision required by SLAM','Detection can contribute information but does not replace the complete estimation/planning system.')])
ask(1942,'systems-demonstrations','Demonstration list and systems-thinking introduction','Which evidence claim is justified by the humanoid, industrial-arm, ROS and mobile-robot demonstrations?', [('They illustrate complementary integrations; they do not prove every possible module combination is correct or safe','Demonstration is bounded evidence, not exhaustive interface and safety validation.'),('A successful demonstration proves every implementation with the same module names will interoperate','Names do not establish matching units, interfaces, timing or assumptions.'),('A ROS communication link alone proves all connected physical dynamics models are correct','Transport connectivity does not validate model correctness.')],conflict=DEMO)
ask(1943,'industry-roles','Robotics Engineer, AI/ML Engineer and Perception Engineer bullets','Which primary-focus mapping follows the lecture’s industry-role list?', [('Robotics engineer: system integration; AI/ML engineer: perception/decision models; perception engineer: vision and SLAM','The source differentiates broad integration, learning-model work and perception specialization.'),('Robotics engineer: only model labeling; AI/ML engineer: only wiring; perception engineer: only torque-loop tuning','These assign narrower unrelated emphases than the listed roles.'),('Robotics engineer: only safety certification; AI/ML engineer: only mechanical machining; perception engineer: only procurement','These replace the listed technical responsibilities.')],kind='match',ass=['Job roles overlap; these are the source-listed emphases, not exclusive hiring definitions or current vacancies.'])
ask(1944,'specialist-roles','Hardware, controls and test/safety role bullets','A project has three tasks: integrate a sensor interface, tune a feedback loop, and validate behavior against safety requirements. Which source-listed specialties align most directly?', [('Embedded/hardware, controls, and robotics test/safety respectively','The source associates these roles with platform interfaces, feedback design and validation.'),('Controls, test/safety, and embedded/hardware respectively','This cyclic shift does not preserve the source’s primary emphases.'),('Test/safety, embedded/hardware, and controls respectively','This alternative shift again misassigns the primary roles.')],kind='match',ass=['This asks primary alignment, not who is forbidden to collaborate on a task.'])
ask(1945,'academic-pathways','Graduate study, research labs and publishing venues bullets','Which route matches the lecture’s research-pathway description?', [('Specialize through graduate study, contribute through a university/industry lab, and communicate research at venues such as ICRA, IROS, RSS, AIR or iNaCoMM','The listed routes combine deeper expertise, research participation and community review.'),('Treat conference attendance alone as equivalent to completing a research degree or publishing reviewed work','Participation, degree completion and publication are distinct achievements.'),('Limit robotics research to one employer type because the lecture excludes university–industry participation','Both university and industry labs are explicitly mentioned.')],ass=['The listed venues and paths are attributed to the lecture; no current schedule, admission promise or vacancy is asserted.'])
ask(1946,'research-pathways','R&D Centers and Interdisciplinary Research bullets','A team develops a compliant robot using new materials and studies human responses. Which research framing matches the source?', [('An interdisciplinary robotics project combining materials and cognitive/human-interaction questions, potentially within mission-driven R&D','The lecture explicitly links robotics with materials, biology and cognitive science and with national/corporate missions.'),('A project outside robotics solely because its material and cognitive questions cross disciplines','The source treats such intersections as robotics research opportunities.'),('A project that can only occur in a university because national or corporate R&D excludes robotics','The source explicitly names national labs and corporate counterparts.')],kind='scenario')
ask(1946,'research-pathways','Postdoctoral Research bullet','What does the lecture support about postdoctoral research?', [('It is a possible path toward a long-term academic robotics/AI career','The source describes a path, not an automatic appointment or universal requirement.'),('It guarantees a permanent faculty position once completed','No employment guarantee is stated.'),('It is presented as mandatory before any industrial robotics engineering role','The slide does not impose that prerequisite on industry careers.')])
ask(1947,'entrepreneurship','Service/inspection and component-startup bullets','A startup sells sensing modules to larger robot-system builders rather than operating an inspection service. Which source distinction describes it?', [('A component-level supplier to robotics integrators','The lecture explicitly includes sensors, actuators and AI modules as component ventures.'),('An inspection-service operator solely because its customers use robots for inspection','The customer’s service does not change the startup’s supplied component into an operated service.'),('A research-conference organizer because the product includes AI','AI content does not define a publishing/community business model.')],kind='scenario')
ask(1947,'entrepreneurship','Funding and technical co-founder bullets','Which inference remains defensible from the lecture’s entrepreneurship discussion?', [('Technical expertise can support a co-founder role, but the slide does not establish a specific venture’s funding or commercial success','The source describes opportunities without a dated grant, business validation or guaranteed outcome.'),('Finishing the course guarantees eligibility for every robotics grant','No program-specific eligibility evidence is supplied.'),('Reported sector growth proves that customer validation is unnecessary for a new robotics product','Aggregate opportunity does not validate a particular market or product.')],conflict=CAREER)
ask(1948,'portfolio-community','Portfolio and open-source contribution bullets','Which portfolio evidence best follows the lecture’s practical guidance?', [('A documented course or personal project plus a traceable contribution to a ROS package or simulator','The source includes concrete projects and open-source work as evidence of practice.'),('A list of framework names with no implemented project or contribution','Naming tools alone does not show the recommended hands-on work.'),('An uncredited copy of an existing repository presented as an original project','Copied claims do not establish the learner’s own technical contribution.')],kind='scenario')
ask(1948,'portfolio-community','Competition and networking bullets','Why does the lecture recommend both robotics competitions and professional/community participation?', [('Competitions expose ideas to engineering constraints; IEEE-RAS, meetups and student programs support professional connection','These are complementary technical-testing and community-building benefits.'),('Competition ranking substitutes for all safety validation, while networking proves code correctness','Neither activity replaces the distinct engineering evidence claimed here.'),('Community membership is itself evidence that a robot has met its physical performance requirements','Membership does not test the robot’s measured requirements.')])
